window.LESSONS["d8-dissection"].chunks.push(
  {
    level: 4,
    title: "Senior reasoning: the type-A operative strategy, decision by decision",
    html: `<p>The junior knows "type A goes to the OR." The senior owns the four strategic decisions that make the operation survivable.</p>
<p><b>1. Cannulation for antegrade true-lumen flow.</b> The overriding goal is to perfuse the <b>true lumen</b> antegrade and avoid pressurizing or expanding the false lumen. <b>Axillary/innominate</b> cannulation (directly or via a side graft) delivers antegrade flow and enables antegrade cerebral perfusion; <b>direct central true-lumen</b> cannulation under TEE/epiaortic guidance is an alternative. <b>Femoral</b> return is a fallback but risks <b>retrograde false-lumen perfusion and malperfusion</b>, so it is chosen deliberately, not by default.</p>
<p><b>2. Temperature and circulatory-arrest strategy.</b> The distal (arch) anastomosis is done under <b>hypothermic circulatory arrest</b> with cerebral protection — <b>unilateral or bilateral antegrade cerebral perfusion</b> (via the axillary/innominate route) is now favored over deep hypothermia alone, allowing more moderate cooling and shorter, safer arrest times; NIRS monitors symmetry.</p>
<p><b>3. Arch extent.</b> Match the resection to the tear. A <b>hemiarch</b> (open distal anastomosis just under the arch) suffices when the primary tear is in the ascending aorta and the arch is not aneurysmal. <b>Total arch ± frozen elephant trunk</b> is reserved for a tear in the arch, an arch aneurysm, or extensive distal disease — trading a bigger operation for a more durable distal result.</p>
<p><b>4. Root management.</b> If the tear spares the root and the leaflets are normal, <b>resuspend the commissures</b> and replace the ascending aorta, preserving the native valve. Replace the root (<b>Bentall</b>, or valve-sparing in a young connective-tissue patient) when the tear extends into the root, the root is aneurysmal or destroyed, or the disease is Marfan/Loeys-Dietz. False-lumen obliteration and a well-constructed distal anastomosis with felt/glue reinforcement complete the repair.</p>`
  },
  {
    level: 5,
    title: "PGY-5 mastery: running the 2 a.m. type-A and rescuing its surprises",
    html: `<p><b>Viva bar:</b> <i>"Type A on CT with inferior ST elevation and a cold, pulseless right leg — what does each finding tell you, what is your cannulation and arch plan, and what do you do if the leg malperfusion persists after you have replaced the ascending aorta?"</i></p>
<p><b>Read the findings.</b> Inferior ST elevation = the dissection flap involves or narrows the <b>right coronary ostium</b> (right-sided malperfusion); the cold pulseless leg = <b>iliofemoral malperfusion</b>, dynamic (flap prolapsing across the true lumen) or static (flap extending into the vessel). <b>Resuscitate to permissive parameters without delaying surgery</b> — this is a surgical emergency where untreated mortality runs roughly 1–2% per hour.</p>
<p><b>The surprises and their rescues:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Persistent malperfusion after a technically complete proximal repair.</b> Central repair fixes <i>dynamic</i> malperfusion by re-pressurizing the true lumen — so if the leg pinks up, it was dynamic. If it stays cold, the branch obstruction is <b>static</b> and needs a <b>directed intervention</b> (fenestration or branch stenting/bypass), not more waiting.</li>
  <li><b>Coronary-ostial involvement.</b> If the RCA ostium is dissected, <b>mobilize a button and reimplant</b> or place a <b>saphenous-vein graft</b> to the RCA rather than hope the resuspension restores flow.</li>
  <li><b>Catastrophic coagulopathic bleeding from friable dissected tissue.</b> Anticipate it: felt/glue-reinforced anastomoses, rewarm fully, and correct with a <b>viscoelastic-guided</b> product strategy (fibrinogen, platelets, factors) — friable tissue plus long bypass and arrest is the perfect coagulopathy storm.</li>
  <li><b>A new post-op neurologic deficit</b> → image and distinguish embolic stroke from malperfusion; it reframes the whole recovery and the surveillance plan.</li>
</ul>
<div class='kbox danger'><div class='kbox-tag'>DANGER</div><p>Never treat a type-A pericardial effusion with pericardiocentesis: draining it restores the pressure that drives fresh rupture. The rescue for tamponade here is the operation — go to the OR.</p></div>`
  }
);

window.LESSONS["d8-pericardium"].chunks.push(
  {
    level: 4,
    title: "Senior reasoning: constriction versus restriction, and the operation each selects",
    html: `<p>The decision that selects the operation is <b>constrictive pericarditis (a surgical disease) versus restrictive cardiomyopathy (a medical one)</b> — they share a congested, low-output picture but demand opposite management.</p>
<p><b>The discriminators.</b> Constriction is a stiff <i>shell</i> around a normal myocardium: <b>Kussmaul's sign</b> (JVP rises with inspiration), <b>ventricular interdependence</b> (respiratory discordance of RV/LV systolic pressures), a <b>preserved or high mitral annular e′</b> (~ &gt; 8–12 cm/s — the "annulus paradoxus"), a septal bounce, and a thick/calcified pericardium on CT. Restriction is stiff <i>muscle</i>: a <b>low e′</b>, no significant interdependence, biatrial enlargement, and often an infiltrative cause. On catheterization both show elevated, equalized diastolic pressures with a dip-and-plateau, so the <b>e′ and the interdependence</b> — not the filling pressures — do the discriminating.</p>
<p><b>The operation.</b> Constriction is treated by <b>pericardiectomy</b> — as complete a resection as safely possible, phrenic-to-phrenic, off the diaphragmatic surface — because a partial resection leaves a constricting rim. The senior warns the family that <b>radiation-induced constriction carries the highest operative risk</b> (associated myocardial fibrosis) and that low output may persist for days after release because the chronically constrained myocardium has little immediate contractile reserve. A tamponade, by contrast, is drained; a localized <b>post-operative</b> clot behind the heart is a <i>surgical</i> re-exploration, not a needle.</p>`
  }
);

/* ---- Cardiac tumours (Ch20 Key Questions) — merged into Day-9 pericardial lesson ---- */
window.LESSONS["d8-pericardium"].chunks.push(
  {
    title: "Cardiac Tumours",
    html: `<p><b>Secondary (metastatic) tumours are ~30× more common than primary</b> cardiac tumours — by haematogenous/lymphatic spread (lymphoma, breast, lung, melanoma, sarcoma), direct extension (oesophagus, lung, breast, thymus), or transvenous growth up the IVC into the right atrium (renal cell, uterine, hepatic, adrenal). Of <b>primary</b> tumours, ~75% are benign and ~25% malignant.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Myxoma</b> — the commonest benign primary tumour in adults (~40%). Incidence ~1:500,000, age 40–60, female (70%), 5% familial. ~<b>75% arise in the left atrium off the interatrial septum</b> (20% RA, 5% ventricular/valvar). Presents with the triad of <b>obstruction</b> (mimics mitral/tricuspid stenosis; a diastolic "tumour plop"), <b>embolism</b>, and <b>constitutional symptoms</b> (fever, weight loss, raised inflammatory markers) — the great mimic. A "wrecking-ball" effect can cause regurgitation.</li>
  <li><b>Papillary fibroelastoma</b> — small tumours with frond-like projections, usually on <b>valves (aortic, mitral)</b>. Often incidental but can <b>embolise</b> (stroke, coronary) or obstruct → resect.</li>
  <li><b>Lipoma</b> — encapsulated, often right atrium; resect if obstructing. <b>Rhabdomyoma</b> — commonest primary tumour in <b>children</b>, multicentric, associated with tuberous sclerosis; may regress.</li>
  <li><b>Malignant:</b> <b>angiosarcoma</b> (commonest primary malignancy; right atrium; usually metastatic at diagnosis, median survival ~6 months) and rhabdomyosarcoma (~12 months). Most sarcomas are incurable; transplantation is considered only without metastatic spread.</li>
</ul>
<div class="kbox pearl">
  <div class="kbox-tag">MYXOMA SURGERY</div>
  <p>Bicaval cannulation with snares and <b>minimal cardiac manipulation</b> to avoid embolising friable tumour. Resect <b>en bloc with a septal margin</b> (patch-close the interatrial septum if involved), irrigate the cavity with saline, and <b>avoid cell-saver/pump-sucker</b> on the tumour field. Assess the valve for a wrecking-ball lesion. Recurrence &lt;5% (inadequate resection, seeding, or multicentric/Carney disease).</p>
</div>
<div class="kbox">
  <div class="kbox-tag">CARNEY COMPLEX</div>
  <p>Autosomal-dominant familial myxoma: younger, male, often <b>biatrial and multicentric (65%)</b> with high recurrence (~40%), plus spotty mucocutaneous pigmentation, adrenocortical nodular disease, and Sertoli-cell testicular tumours.</p>
</div>`
  },
  {
    title: "Cardiac Trauma",
    html: `<p>Cardiac trauma runs on ATLS priorities, with specific attention to <b>tamponade, dysrhythmia, contusion, and the aortic isthmus</b>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Blunt cardiac injury:</b> myocardial contusion (serial ECG/troponin/echo, monitor in HDU for dysrhythmia), and structural injury — pleuropericardial tears, RA disruption at the caval junctions, traumatic ASD/VSD, or acute AR.</li>
  <li><b>Penetrating cardiac injury:</b> diagnose tamponade fast (<b>Beck's triad</b> — hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds — plus pulsus paradoxus/Kussmaul and echo). Decompress and repair: median sternotomy if time allows (best access to great vessels), otherwise a <b>left anterolateral thoracotomy</b> in extremis. Suture lacerations directly, buttressed with Teflon strips; ligate/graft injured coronaries; beware air embolism when lifting the heart to reach a posterior wound.</li>
  <li><b>Resuscitative (ED) thoracotomy:</b> indicated per ATLS for <b>penetrating</b> (not blunt) trauma with loss of output within ~10 min of PEA — left anterolateral thoracotomy for pericardial evacuation, open cardiac massage, and descending-aortic cross-clamp.</li>
</ul>
<div class="kbox danger">
  <div class="kbox-tag">TRAUMATIC AORTIC RUPTURE (ISTHMUS)</div>
  <p>A deceleration injury tears the aorta where it is tethered — the <b>isthmus (just distal to the left subclavian, at the ligamentum arteriosum) in 80–90%</b>. ~80–90% die at the scene; of survivors, ~50% die within 48 h. Suspect it with 1st–3rd rib/sternal/scapular fractures and a <b>widened mediastinum (&gt;8 cm)</b> / lost aortic knob / left apical cap on CXR; confirm with CT. Keep <b>SBP &lt;110 mmHg</b> while treating other injuries. Repair by endovascular stent (TEVAR) or open interposition graft using clamp-and-sew (paraplegia risk if clamp &gt;30 min), a Gott shunt, or left-heart bypass for distal perfusion.</p>
</div>`
  }
);
